 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 6,
   "text": "MALHAM COVE. A curved cliff 80 metres high, made of limestone laid down when this was a warm tropical sea. After the Ice Age a waterfall taller than Niagara poured over the lip. Today the top is dry limestone pavement."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 5,
   "text": "LIMESTONE PAVEMENT. The cracks are called grykes and the blocks are clints. Ferns grow down in the grykes where it is sheltered. If you can identify a clint without a guidebook, you may call yourself a geologist."
  },
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE YORKSHIRE DALES. Green valleys, grey walls, and a great many sheep with opinions. Welcome west. The tea shops are about two miles apart, which is the proper distance."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "A DRYSTONE WALL. Built with no mortar, only skill and a good eye. There are about 5,000 miles of them in the Dales, and every one is held up by gravity and pride. Please close the gate."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "AYSGARTH FALLS. Three sets of falls on the River Ure, stepping down over limestone ledges. This is where the film of the outlaw of Sherwood was shot in 1990. The Dales will play any forest if asked nicely."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "WENSLEYDALE. Famous for a crumbly white cheese, which a certain inventor with a dog loves. It goes very well with fruit cake. This is not a joke. It is a Yorkshire tradition."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "FIELD BARNS. The Dales have thousands of small stone barns, one to a field. Each kept hay for a few cows through the winter. Now they keep views for the walkers, and the occasional ewe."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "GORDALE SCAR. A gorge where the cliffs close in until they nearly touch overhead, and a waterfall comes down the middle. Turner sketched it and Wordsworth wrote a sonnet about it. The beck did not comment."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "yorkshire_dales_norse",
   "people": "norse",
   "title": "Dales Words",
   "text": "The Dales map speaks Old Norse, left by Viking settlers: a dale (dalr) is a valley, a beck (bekkr) a stream, a fell (fjall) a hill, a gill (gil) a ravine, and a force (fors) a waterfall. A walk here is a vocabulary test with stiles."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "yorkshire_dales_swaledale",
   "title": "The Swaledale Sheep",
   "text": "The Swaledale is a hardy hill sheep with a black face, a white nose and curling horns, bred for wet winters on the high fells. A Swaledale ram's head is the badge of the Yorkshire Dales National Park, which tells you who really runs the place."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 5,
   "plaque": "landsky_pavement_orders",
   "title": "Limestone pavement, protected",
   "text": "This pavement began as the floor of a tropical sea 330 million years ago. Ice scraped it bare, and since the ice left, about 12,000 years ago, rain made slightly acid by the air has been eating down the cracks, so that the blocks (clints) stand apart from the fissures (grykes). A pavement takes thousands of years and was being carted off for garden rockeries, so since 1981 the law has let councils protect them with Limestone Pavement Orders. Look, photograph, leave."
  }
 ],
